  "query_preview": "..The surface ridge of high pressure over the eastern \nGulf of Alaska looks to persist through the extended forecast \nperiod. This will keep the heavy rain threat on the west side of \nthe ridge axis and outside of our forecast area. However, marine \nlayer clouds will likely be held under that ridge over the gulf \nand along the outer coast. As a mid-level trough passes over the \nmarine layer Tuesday night thru Wednesday the PVA will be just \nenough lift to have a chance/slight chance of rain (more likely \ndrizzle) along the northern and central outer coast and into the \nIcy Strait corridor. The ridge will periodically flatten some \n(dirty ridge) and allow moisture/clouds to stream in, namely for \nthe Yakutat area. Meanwhile, areas on the lee side of the ridge \naxis (Chatham Strait to Clarence Strait) will have NWLY \n(downslope) flow and mostly clear skies for majority of the next \nweek. \n\nMost models were in lock-step today on keeping the ridge offshore \nfor the work week and sharper as well. The GFS represented these \nwind directions well with NWLY flow along the southern outer coast \nand southern inner channels to Dixon Entrance as well as stronger \nwind speeds. These speeds were raised in the forecast to about 15-\n20kt, but some areas of 25kt are possible in vicinity of Cape \nDecision to Dixon Entrance and will be monitored over the coming \ndays.",
